1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:07,302
The electromagnetic spectrum is the map every wireless signal in the world lives on.

2
00:00:07,302 --> 00:00:12,162
Right now, passing straight through you, are broadcast radio, aviation voice,

3
00:00:12,162 --> 00:00:17,401
police dispatch, satellite downlinks, and the light from your screen — thousands of

4
00:00:17,401 --> 00:00:20,367
signals sharing the same air without colliding.

5
00:00:20,367 --> 00:00:20,619
How?

6
00:00:20,619 --> 00:00:24,406
They're all the same physical thing, at different addresses.

7
00:00:24,406 --> 00:00:27,310
Sorting out those addresses is the whole idea.

8
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:32,701
The electromagnetic spectrum is the full range of electromagnetic radiation,

9
00:00:32,701 --> 00:00:37,772
ordered by frequency: from radio waves through microwaves and visible light to

10
00:00:37,772 --> 00:00:39,202
X-rays and gamma rays.

11
00:00:39,202 --> 00:00:44,208
One continuous whole — the named regions are human labels of convenience, not

12
00:00:44,208 --> 00:00:45,508
physical boundaries.

13
00:00:45,958 --> 00:00:51,247
Here's the surprising part: radio waves, the light you see, and a hospital X-ray

14
00:00:51,247 --> 00:00:52,503
are one phenomenon.

15
00:00:52,503 --> 00:00:57,791
Each is an oscillating electric and magnetic field propagating through space — a

16
00:00:57,791 --> 00:01:02,948
changing electric field creates a magnetic field, and vice versa, and the pair

17
00:01:02,948 --> 00:01:04,666
sustains itself as a wave.

18
00:01:04,666 --> 00:01:08,302
One law, Maxwell's equations, governs the entire strip.

19
00:01:08,302 --> 00:01:13,657
The only thing that changes as you slide along it is how fast the field vibrates.

20
00:01:14,107 --> 00:01:19,316
And that one variable changes everything, because the energy each cycle carries

21
00:01:19,316 --> 00:01:20,700
rises with frequency.

22
00:01:20,700 --> 00:01:26,107
At the top of the strip, gamma rays vibrate so fast their photons are ionising and

23
00:01:26,107 --> 00:01:26,766
dangerous.

24
00:01:26,766 --> 00:01:31,777
Down at the radio end, the photons are far too weak to break chemical bonds.

25
00:01:31,777 --> 00:01:36,129
Same wave, wildly different character — set entirely by frequency.

26
00:01:36,579 --> 00:01:41,830
Radio is the low-frequency end — conventionally about three kilohertz to three

27
00:01:41,830 --> 00:01:47,215
hundred gigahertz — and it's special for an engineering reason: the oscillations

28
00:01:47,215 --> 00:01:52,332
are slow enough that ordinary electronic circuits — oscillators, amplifiers,

29
00:01:52,332 --> 00:01:56,303
antennas — can generate, radiate, and detect them directly.

30
00:01:56,303 --> 00:02:01,487
Above radio, into infrared and light, no circuit switches fast enough, and we

31
00:02:01,487 --> 00:02:03,170
resort to optics instead.

32
00:02:03,170 --> 00:02:08,758
The radio portion is itself subdivided by the ITU into named bands — VLF up through

33
00:02:08,758 --> 00:02:12,393
EHF — each one a decade of frequency, a factor of ten.

34
00:02:12,843 --> 00:02:18,027
Where a signal sits on this map decides almost everything practical about it.

35
00:02:18,027 --> 00:02:23,413
Propagation: HF waves, three to thirty megahertz, refract off the ionosphere and

36
00:02:23,413 --> 00:02:28,799
can travel worldwide, while VHF and UHF are largely line-of-sight and microwaves

37
00:02:28,799 --> 00:02:30,953
are blocked by terrain and rain.

38
00:02:30,953 --> 00:02:35,801
Antenna size: a resonant antenna is a fraction of a wavelength, so lower

39
00:02:35,801 --> 00:02:38,898
frequencies demand physically larger antennas.

40
00:02:38,898 --> 00:02:44,486
And bandwidth: higher bands have more absolute spectrum to spare — the reason Wi-Fi

41
00:02:44,486 --> 00:02:45,900
and 5G keep climbing.

42
00:02:45,900 --> 00:02:51,286
One sense of scale: the visible-light octave alone spans more frequency than the

43
00:02:51,286 --> 00:02:53,171
entire radio range below it.

44
00:02:53,171 --> 00:02:57,009
Radio is a small, crowded strip of an enormous continuum.

45
00:02:57,459 --> 00:03:00,890
Crowded enough that it's governed like territory.

46
00:03:00,890 --> 00:03:06,563
The ITU and national regulators — the FCC, Ofcom, and others — allocate slices of

47
00:03:06,563 --> 00:03:11,535
the radio spectrum to services, so a given frequency legally belongs to

48
00:03:11,535 --> 00:03:15,247
broadcasting, aviation, land-mobile radio, and so on.

49
00:03:15,247 --> 00:03:19,449
A frequency isn't just a physical address; it's a legal one.

50
00:03:19,899 --> 00:03:26,502
GopherTrunk's spectrum and waterfall view is a window a few megahertz wide onto

51
00:03:26,502 --> 00:03:32,854
exactly this map, bounded by what the hardware can reach — an RTL-SDR covers

52
00:03:32,854 --> 00:03:39,374
roughly 24 megahertz to 1.7 gigahertz, a wideband device like the HackRF up to

53
00:03:39,374 --> 00:03:40,878
about 6 gigahertz.

54
00:03:40,878 --> 00:03:47,648
The trunking systems it decodes — P25, DMR, NXDN, TETRA — live in the VHF and UHF

55
00:03:47,648 --> 00:03:54,168
land-mobile bands, typically 136 to 174, 380 to 520, and 700 to 900 megahertz.

56
00:03:54,168 --> 00:04:01,105
Where a target sits in the spectrum dictates the antenna, how far it will be heard,

57
00:04:01,105 --> 00:04:04,114
and which SDR can receive it at all.

58
00:04:04,564 --> 00:04:10,311
So: one continuous spectrum, one kind of wave, and a signal's address on it decides

59
00:04:10,311 --> 00:04:14,605
how it travels, what receives it, and who's allowed to use it.

60
00:04:14,605 --> 00:04:16,474
Full write-up linked below.

